From 096c7ec108cd7e0ef720c79b3b4381681c698b56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 11:17:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0127 -- run-based masked sprite blit,
 PIXEL_PTR as a typedef

Consolidates 0127, 0160, 0161.

Why. The masked blit ran at 19.4 cycles/pixel against a measured 7.8 copy
ceiling, paying pointer increments, a bounds test and a branch per pixel.
Bulk-copying opaque runs amortises that, but only if runs are long, so the
run length was measured first:
- 0127: opaque-run statistics, compiled only under DOSAGS_SPRITE_RUN_STATS
  because it puts three adds and a branch in the hottest loop in the engine.
  Result recorded in the source: mean opaque run 57.0 px (1,309,835 runs over
  74.6M opaque pixels) against a decision rule of 16 written before the data.
- 0160: the run-based blit. One memcpy per opaque run. Default ON;
  DOSAGS_RUNBLIT=0 falls back to the per-pixel loop so both arms live in one
  binary. The run's byte length comes from pointer difference, not sizeof,
  because cspr.h is instantiated at 8/15/16/24/32 bpp and at 24 bpp a pixel
  is three bytes.
- 0161: PIXEL_PTR becomes a typedef in each cdefs header. As a macro,
  "PIXEL_PTR a = x, b = y;" declares b as a plain integer; a typedef
  distributes across declarators. Safe because each translation unit includes
  exactly one cdefs header.

Behaviour change: the run-based blit is ON by default. The record puts its
real gain at 0.48 ms per tick, not the 2.44 ms first modelled, because the
sprites are speckled.

Provenance of this export (2026-09-21): these edits sat uncommitted in the
vendor tree from patch 0123 onward, which is why every gate cell since said
build_dirty=1. They were made under one campaign-wide patch counter shared
with vendor/SDL, and each hunk carries its number in an in-source comment.
Intermediate states were never committed, so this patch is a hunk-level
partition of the final tree by concern, attributed by those comments -- not a
replay of the order the edits were made in, and its intermediate state was not
built on its own. The numbers named below are the ones found in its hunks.

